=== layout: section

# Human leukocyte antigen (HLA) typing

=== layout: two-panels

# HLA Genes

@left

- Cell-surface proteins are responsible for the regulation of the immune system in humans.
- HLA genes are highly polymorphic, which means that they have many different alleles.
- They are the major cause of organ transplant rejections.

@right

![Slide image 8](assets/lect03b-blood_hla_typing/image_008.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# HLA genes are close together

@body

![Slide image 9](assets/lect03b-blood_hla_typing/image_009.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# Inheritance of HLA Haplotypes

@left

![Slide image 10](assets/lect03b-blood_hla_typing/image_010.png)

@right

- Child 2 and Child 4 have matching HLA haplotypes.
- Each parent passes one of two haplotypes: four combinations.
- Siblings have a 1 in 4 (25%) chance of matching.
- Identical twins usually match; rare recombination can change this.

=== layout: one-panel

# Inheritance of HLA Haplotypes

@body

- Father has haplotypes FL and FR.
- Mother has haplotypes ML and MR.
- Each child receives one haplotype from each parent.
- How many combinations are possible?

